+// Time-trial rankings from mkwleaderboards.com for the MKW-SP Rankings fork.
+//
+// The fork (tools/rankings.js) restores the vanilla Mario Kart Channel ranking pages inside MKW-SP's
+// Rankings section: the top-10 download page (0xAE), the top-10 list (0xAF, RankingDetailPage) and
+// the champion ghost download in ChannelStartTimeTrial (0xAC). Those pages talk to ServerMgr, which
+// drives a small ranking client (instance at 0x809C2140) that used to query Nintendo's
+// NintendoRacingService over DWC/GameSpy. That client is reimplemented natively here:
+//
+//   0x806773C8  setup(client, profile, region, flag)          region byte at +0x0C (matching area + 1)
+//   0x806776B0  requestTopTen(client, course, 0, world, dest)  dest = {u32 count; 10 x 84-byte entry}
+//   0x80677758  requestGhost(client, course, world, buf, size) buf = raw RKG (0x2800 bytes), *size
+//   0x80677240  process(client)                                 called by ServerMgr::Update every frame
+//
+// Client fields: +0x00 state (1 ready, 2 busy), +0x04 result (1 ok, 3 error), +0x08 error code,
+// +0x10 request kind, +0x18 destination, +0x1C size pointer. ServerMgr then validates every Mii
+// (RFL::CheckValidRaw), stores the champion in the license and builds the ghost exactly as it did
+// for Nintendo's data, so the game's own screens show the result.
+//
+// A top-10 entry is { u32 time_ms; RFLStoreData mii (76 bytes); u8 controller; u8 region; u8 0;
+// u8 country }: the Wii country code drives the flag, controller 0 shows the Wii Wheel icon. Data comes from
+// https://mkwleaderboards.com/api: the category list of the track, then every approved run of that
+// category (sorted by time), reduced to each player's best and filtered by continent for Regional.
+// Miis come from the runs' ghost files (chadsoft or the site), renamed to the site's player name.
+
